Use two pointers to traverse an array or string efficiently,
usually reducing O(n²) solutions to O(n).

Template Code:
def two_pointer(arr, target):
    l, r = 0, len(arr) - 1
    while l < r:
        s = arr[l] + arr[r]
        if s == target:
            return [l, r]
        elif s < target:
            l += 1
        else:
            r -= 1
    return [-1, -1]

Time: O(n) single pass after sort
Space: O(1) extra

Common patterns
Pattern	Pointer movement	Example
Opposite ends --	left = 0, right = n-1 --	Reverse Array, Valid Palindrome
Same direction --	Both move forward --	Remove Duplicates, Move Zeroes
Sorted array --	Move pointer based on comparison --	Two Sum II, Squares of Sorted Array
Fix + two pointers --	Fix one element, use two pointers on the rest	3Sum, 3Sum Closest
Three pointers --	Track three regions or positions --	Sort Colors
Two boundaries --	Move the shorter boundary --	Container With Most Water
Running maximums --	Track left/right maximums	-- Trapping Rain Water

